WEBVTT

00:00:00.127 --> 00:00:03.259
{happy}Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:05.277
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:05.277 --> 00:00:10.104
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

00:00:10.942 --> 00:00:13.629
Here is a number worth remembering:

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:14.467
five per cent.

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:19.285
That is roughly what the United States government has recently been paying to borrow

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:21.315
money for ten years.

00:00:21.315 --> 00:00:23.058
And it is not only America:

00:00:23.058 --> 00:00:24.314
Japan is paying more too.

00:00:24.314 --> 00:00:24.928
Why?

00:00:26.346 --> 00:00:30.569
I'm Mittal Shah, and in the next few minutes I'll make the bond market feel a lot

00:00:30.569 --> 00:00:32.856
less mysterious.

00:00:32.856 --> 00:00:34.550
{fluent}Let's start with what happened.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:39.532
On the tenth of November, the United States reported its inflation figures for

00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:40.817
October, and they were hot.

00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:46.244
On the same day, the yield on the ten-year government bond jumped by about a tenth

00:00:46.244 --> 00:00:50.866
of a percentage point, to about five per cent, according to market reports.

00:00:51.587 --> 00:00:53.080
Now, a quick word on that yield.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.642
A government bond is simply an I-O-U:

00:00:57.642 --> 00:01:02.933
you lend the government money today, and it promises to pay you interest and give

00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:04.240
your money back later.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.948
The yield is the interest a buyer earns on that bond, given the price they pay.

00:01:07.948 --> 00:01:10.587
So when you hear "yields rose", read it as:

00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:15.892
lending to the government now pays lenders more, and costs the government more.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.762
So why do yields move at all?

00:01:18.762 --> 00:01:20.025
Think of it as a seesaw.

00:01:20.025 --> 00:01:23.635
Imagine an old bond that pays four dollars a year on a hundred dollars.

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:26.585
Then new bonds start paying five dollars a year.

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:30.036
Few buyers will pay a hundred dollars for the old one.

00:01:30.036 --> 00:01:35.804
Its price falls to roughly eighty dollars, until four dollars a year is a five per

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.289
cent yield for the new buyer.

00:01:37.289 --> 00:01:38.121
Price down, yield up.

00:01:38.761 --> 00:01:40.615
And that brings us to why yields went up.

00:01:40.615 --> 00:01:42.882
One likely reason is inflation.

00:01:42.882 --> 00:01:49.114
If prices are rising faster, a lender wants more interest, because the money they

00:01:49.114 --> 00:01:50.069
get back will buy less.

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:54.576
The Fed, the United States central bank, has a short-term interest rate of about

00:01:54.576 --> 00:01:56.097
three point nine per cent.

00:01:56.097 --> 00:02:01.421
A ten-year yield at five per cent suggests that lenders want a good deal more than

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.303
that for locking their money away for a decade.

00:02:04.303 --> 00:02:08.482
That reading is our inference, not something anyone has announced.

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:11.857
Here is where the story gets bigger.

00:02:11.857 --> 00:02:14.688
This is not only an American story.

00:02:14.688 --> 00:02:18.534
In Japan, yields have been far lower than in America.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:24.268
On the fifth of November, Japan sold ten-year bonds at an average yield of about two

00:02:24.268 --> 00:02:25.212
point eight per cent.

00:02:25.212 --> 00:02:30.309
And in July, that yield briefly touched about two point nine, the highest since

00:02:30.309 --> 00:02:32.668
November nineteen ninety-six.

00:02:32.668 --> 00:02:38.001
Reports at the time pointed to higher oil prices, bigger government spending plans,

00:02:38.001 --> 00:02:41.284
and doubts about when the Bank of Japan would raise its own rates.

00:02:42.087 --> 00:02:45.232
So why do these markets move together?

00:02:45.232 --> 00:02:49.000
Because big borrowers compete for the same pool of money.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.181
If lenders can earn five per cent in America, then other governments and companies

00:02:55.181 --> 00:02:57.316
have to offer more to keep their buyers.

00:02:57.316 --> 00:02:59.718
That is a reasonable reading, though not a rule.

00:02:59.718 --> 00:03:01.636
And there is a cost:

00:03:01.636 --> 00:03:06.569
the more a government pays in interest, the less room it has for other spending, and

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:11.766
loans for companies and families are usually priced off these same yields.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:14.253
What could this mean for India?

00:03:14.253 --> 00:03:15.365
Possibly this:

00:03:15.365 --> 00:03:21.130
when lending to the American government pays this much, some global money might

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:25.215
prefer that safe return to riskier markets, including the rupee and Indian shares.

00:03:25.215 --> 00:03:28.118
We want to be careful here.

00:03:28.118 --> 00:03:32.804
That is a possibility, not something we have measured today.

00:03:33.618 --> 00:03:35.072
Now, what do we not know?

00:03:35.072 --> 00:03:39.331
These numbers are from the ninth and tenth of November, and yields change every day.

00:03:39.331 --> 00:03:42.594
One hot inflation report does not make a trend.

00:03:42.594 --> 00:03:47.327
We do not know whether inflation will keep running hot, or what central banks will

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:47.927
do next.

00:03:47.858 --> 00:03:49.981
Those are the things to watch.

00:03:50.918 --> 00:03:51.793
To sum up:

00:03:51.793 --> 00:03:53.127
a yield is the price of money.

00:03:53.127 --> 00:03:59.584
In the biggest economies, that price has gone up, and when the price of money rises

00:03:59.584 --> 00:04:03.696
in the biggest economies, it rarely stays at home.

00:04:03.696 --> 00:04:06.982
{happy}Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

